(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-medical-imaging-quiz"]={"title":"Principles of Medical Imaging Quiz 1 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Who is credited with developing the first computed tomography scanner, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Sir Godfrey Hounsfield, who won the 1979 Nobel Prize in Medicine along with Allan Cormack","Wilhelm Roentgen, who won the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901","Allan Cormack alone, who won the 1979 Nobel Prize for Physics","Paul Lauterbur, who won the Nobel Prize for developing magnetic resonance imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the unit of attenuation is named after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does fluoroscopy allow that a conventional radiograph does not?","choices":["Real-time visualisation, allowing evaluation of motion and of positional change in bones and joints","Imaging without any ionizing radiation at all, making it entirely safe to use in pregnancy","Cross-sectional imaging in any geometric plane after acquisition","Differentiation of soft tissues superior to computed tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — images can be viewed live and captured as stills or video. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the midsagittal and parasagittal planes?","choices":["Midsagittal runs across the shoulders; parasagittal sections lie anterior and posterior","Midsagittal runs at the level of the heart; parasagittal sections are angled off the axis","Midsagittal runs in the body's midline; parasagittal sections lie to either side of it","Midsagittal runs through the umbilicus; parasagittal sections lie above and below it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the midsagittal plane is also called the median plane. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What should you do if you are unsure which modality to order?","choices":["Order both the plain film and the cross-sectional study at the same visit","Tell the radiologist what you are looking for, and they can guide the choice","Order the study with the widest range of densities, and narrow down afterwards","Order the study with the lowest radiation dose, and escalate only if it is negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the question you are asking is the useful thing to communicate. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the deck give as the key takeaway on contrast safety?","choices":["Always obtain written consent and give a prophylactic antihistamine","Always ask about allergies and assess kidney function","Always withhold contrast when any drug allergy is documented","Always use the iso-osmolal agent regardless of the indication or the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and pre-treatment exists when contrast is absolutely necessary. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which advantages are named for computed tomography?","choices":["It expands the grey scale beyond the five basic densities, reduces overlapping of structures, works with implantable devices, and allows three-dimensional reconstruction","It uses no ionizing radiation at all and is described as being the safest of all of the imaging modalities that are currently available anywhere","It is fully portable, inexpensive, and can be brought to the patient's bedside","It is superior to magnetic resonance imaging for differentiating soft tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is described as the cornerstone of cross-sectional imaging. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the deck summarise ultrasound's safety?","choices":["Safe once screened, though implanted metallic devices must be excluded beforehand","Very safe, without any known major side effects at medically diagnostic levels","Broadly safe, though a cumulative dose limit applies across repeated examinations","Safe in adults, though it is avoided in pregnancy and in infants as a precaution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is called the safest of these modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the deck say about shellfish allergy and iodinated contrast?","choices":["There should be no cross-reactivity between shellfish and iodinated radiocontrast","There is well-established cross-reactivity, so the two allergies must be treated as one","There is partial cross-reactivity, so contrast may be given at a reduced concentration","There is cross-reactivity only with the iso-osmolal agents such as iodixanol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the cross-reactivity idea is explicitly rejected. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What are technetium-99's stated indications, and how does it distribute?","choices":["Bone scans, myocardial perfusion, functional brain imaging, immunoscintigraphy and sentinel node identification; it travels to areas of higher blood flow and cellular activity","Cancer staging, brain disorders and cardiac blood flow, together with tumour delineation and lesion characterisation; it concentrates wherever the glucose consumption of the tissue is highest","Vasculopathy, emboli, thrombi, stenosis and aneurysm assessment, along with graft surveillance; it fills the vascular lumen and is imaged during the first pass through it","Central nervous system tumours, metastases and soft tissue masses; it shortens relaxation times in the tissue it reaches"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a white blood cell uptake scan is also named. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which programmable magnetic resonance technique is named as useful in stroke?","choices":["Fat-suppressed imaging, which removes the signal from adipose tissue in the field","Post-processing windowing, which re-displays the raw data over a narrower range","Gadolinium-enhanced imaging, which highlights vascular structures after injection","Diffusion-weighted imaging, which evaluates the diffusion of water within tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the scanner can also be programmed to evaluate blood velocity. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How are patients positioned for ultrasound examinations?","choices":["A single standardised position for every study, with the transducer angle changed instead","Standing for all abdominal work and seated for all cardiac work, without exception","Prone for all studies, so that the transducer can be applied along the posterior surface","Dozens of positions depending on the structure and the complaint, most often some form of supine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — head position varies, and the position follows what is being examined. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How are radiographic projections named?","choices":["In the direction the patient faces, from the side nearest the film to the side furthest away","In the order the structures appear, from the most superficial down to the deepest tissue","In the position of the detector, from the cassette's near edge to its furthest corner","In the direction the beam travels, from what it strikes first to the most distal portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — so posterior-anterior means the beam enters the back and exits the front. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which imaging devices does the deck flag as the highest-emitting currently in existence?","choices":["Computed tomography, positron emission tomography and single photon emission tomography","Conventional radiography, fluoroscopy and the angiographic studies performed with x-rays","Magnetic resonance imaging, magnetic resonance angiography and magnetic resonance venography","Ultrasonography, colour Doppler ultrasonography and the hand-held bedside ultrasound units"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck marks this IMPORTANT, and all three use ionizing radiation. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which density is described as the most dense naturally occurring material?","choices":["Fluid","Fat","Calcium","Metal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it absorbs most x-rays, though metal absorbs essentially all of them. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which agent is used for fluoroscopic swallow studies, and how is the study performed?","choices":["Oral gadolinium, with sequential magnetic resonance images taken as the patient swallows","Intravenous iohexol, with sequential computed tomography images taken during swallowing","Oral technetium, with sequential gamma camera images taken as the patient swallows","Oral barium, with sequential x-rays taken as the patient swallows it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — real-time imaging is what makes fluoroscopy suit a swallow study. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which two of the five basic densities cannot be distinguished from one another on a conventional radiograph?","choices":["Calcium and metal, which both absorb all of the beam","Fat and calcium, which occupy adjacent points on the scale","Fluid and soft tissue, which have the same density","Fat and air, which both appear black on the image"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that limitation is one reason computed tomography expands the grey scale. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Who discovered x-rays, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Allan Cormack, in South Africa in 1963, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics","Marie Curie, in France in 1898, awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903","Wilhelm Roentgen, in Germany in 1895, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901","Godfrey Hounsfield, in Britain in 1972, awarded the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1979"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the technique is still called roentgenography after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do the terms radiolucent and hypodense describe?","choices":["A region of increased uptake of an injected radioisotope on nuclear imaging","A darker image, from greater passage of x-ray photons through the tissue and therefore less absorption","A whiter image, from less passage of x-ray photons through the tissue and therefore greater absorption","A brighter signal on a T2-weighted magnetic resonance image of the same tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — radiopaque and hyperdense describe the opposite. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the deck call the most important thing to remember about ultrasound orientation?","choices":["Which frequency is selected: high for superficial structures, low for deeper structures","Where the indicator is: on the right of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the left for everything else","Where the indicator is: on the left of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the right for everything else","How deep the focus is set: shallow for superficial structures, deep for abdominal structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is called crucial, because the whole image flips with it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What does an abdominal series evaluate, and how is the patient positioned?","choices":["The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient standing, to assess air-fluid levels or free air","The genitourinary tract, with the patient supine, to assess stones within the collecting system","The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient supine, to assess bowel wall thickness and oedema","The retroperitoneum, with the patient in decubitus, to assess the psoas shadows and the flanks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — upright is what makes free air and air-fluid levels visible. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which agent is named for intravenous contrast in computed tomography, and what is it?","choices":["Visipaque (iodixanol), a barium salt suspension given into the vein","Gadolinium, a paramagnetic metal chelate given into the vein","Gastrografin, a water-soluble iodinated agent given into the vein","Omnipaque (iohexol), a radioactive form of iodine given into the vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same agent is used, at higher concentration, for angiography. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which vascular magnetic resonance studies are performed without contrast?","choices":["Coronary angiography and retrograde pyelography","Arthrography and intrathecal myelography","Magnetic resonance angiography and magnetic resonance venography","Computed tomography angiography and computed tomography venography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are done without a contrast agent. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which tissues is magnetic resonance imaging described as the best modality for?","choices":["Metabolically active tissue, essentially tumour and infection, with high functional detail","Soft tissue, essentially anything other than bone, with extremely high anatomical detail","Bone and calcified structures, essentially anything mineralised, with high spatial detail","Air-containing structures, essentially the lungs and sinuses, with high contrast detail"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck states it in exactly those terms. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does a kidney-ureter-bladder film evaluate, and how is the patient positioned?","choices":["The genitourinary tract, with the patient supine and the film shot anterior-posterior","The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient standing and the film shot anterior-posterior","The genitourinary tract, with the patient standing and the film shot posterior-anterior","The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient supine and the film shot posterior-anterior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — supine and anterior-posterior distinguishes it from the abdominal series. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does increased attenuation mean on a computed tomography image, and how does it appear?","choices":["A substance producing no signal at all, appearing as a signal void","A region taking up more injected radioisotope than surrounding tissue","A denser substance with a high computed tomography number, appearing whiter","A less dense substance with a low computed tomography number, appearing blacker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same substances would be radiopaque on a conventional radiograph. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which term names an angiographic study of the veins rather than the arteries?","choices":["Doppler study, performed as either a colour Doppler examination or a duplex examination","Coronary angiogram, performed as either a diagnostic catheterisation or an intervention","Arthrogram, performed as either a computed tomography arthrogram or a magnetic one","Venogram, performed as either a magnetic resonance venogram or a computed tomography venogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same modalities, aimed at the venous side. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How is a conventional radiograph produced?","choices":["A magnetic field aligns hydrogen atoms, which emit radio waves when the field is released","A gamma camera detects photons emitted by an injected radioisotope within the patient","Ionizing radiation and light strike a photosensitive surface, producing a latent image that is then processed to become visible","High-frequency sound waves bounce off the tissue and return to a transducer, which then builds up the whole image"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it requires a source, a method to record the image, and a way to process it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In which patients is ultrasound named as often the first study of choice?","choices":["Adult chest and geriatric patients, and it is also used to screen for fractures","Head-injured and intoxicated patients, and it is also used to exclude a bleed","Oncology and post-operative patients, and it is also used to stage known disease","Female pelvis and paediatric patients, and it is also used to guide procedures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is the reason it leads in both groups. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does ultrasonography generate an image?","choices":["High-metabolism tissues concentrate injected tracer, which emits gamma radiation","High-frequency sound waves leave the transducer, bounce off tissue and return to it","High-energy x-ray photons leave the tube, pass through tissue and strike a detector","High-strength magnetic fields align hydrogen, which emits radio waves on release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the transducer is both the source and the receiver. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the stated risks of fluorodeoxyglucose-18?","choices":["Frequent anaphylaxis requiring pre-treatment, and it may cause hyperglycaemia","No contraindications and no known nephrotoxicity, though it may cause hyperglycaemia","No contraindications and no known nephrotoxicity, though it may cause hypoglycaemia","Significant nephrotoxicity requiring pre-hydration, and it may cause hyperglycaemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is renally cleared, so the urinary tract is always contrast positive. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 55"}]};
